---
import { cta } from '../data/site';
---
<section id="faq" class="py-16 sm:py-20">
  <div class="wrap grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-[1fr_1.7fr] lg:gap-12">
    <div data-reveal class="lg:sticky lg:top-24 lg:self-start">
      <p class="chip">FAQ</p>
      <h2 class="mt-4 text-[clamp(1.6rem,3vw,2.25rem)] leading-tight">Questions I Hear Often</h2>
      <p class="mt-3 text-muted">
        Some appeals move quickly. Appellate rules are technical. These answers cover a few of the most common questions clients ask when they are deciding what to do next.
      </p>
      <div class="r mt-6 bg-deep p-5 text-on-deep">
        <p class="tab-label text-hi">Strict deadlines. Careful review.</p>
        <p class="mt-2 font-serif text-lg leading-snug">If you are on trial, make sure you have a court reporter preserve the record.</p>
      </div>
      <a href={cta.href} class="btn btn-accent mt-4 w-full sm:w-auto">{cta.label}</a>
    </div>
    <div data-reveal style="--i:1" class="space-y-2.5">
        <details class="faq r border border-line bg-card">
          <summary class="flex min-h-14 cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 px-5 py-4 font-serif text-lg leading-snug">
            <span>Can I appeal just because I am unhappy with the judge's decision?</span><span class="faq-icon shrink-0 text-accent-text" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </summary>
          <p class="max-w-[40rem] px-5 pb-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted sm:text-base">No. An appeal is not a "do-over" or a second chance to present new evidence. To win an appeal, we must prove that the trial judge made a specific legal error, improperly weighed the evidence, misapplied the law, or erred in a way that significantly impacted the outcome of your case, which is contrary to the law.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq r border border-line bg-card">
          <summary class="flex min-h-14 cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 px-5 py-4 font-serif text-lg leading-snug">
            <span>How long do I have to file an appeal?</span><span class="faq-icon shrink-0 text-accent-text" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </summary>
          <p class="max-w-[40rem] px-5 pb-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted sm:text-base">The window to file a Notice of Appeal is strictly limited, often 30 days or less from the date the judgment is entered, depending on the Supreme Court Rule giving a reviewing court jurisdiction. If you miss this deadline, you could forfeit your right to appeal forever. It is critical to consult an appellate attorney immediately after an adverse ruling.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq r border border-line bg-card">
          <summary class="flex min-h-14 cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 px-5 py-4 font-serif text-lg leading-snug">
            <span>My ex-spouse is appealing a judgment I won. Do I need an appellate attorney?</span><span class="faq-icon shrink-0 text-accent-text" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </summary>
          <p class="max-w-[40rem] px-5 pb-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted sm:text-base">Defending a judgment on appeal requires the same specialized legal research and brief-writing skills as filing one. You need an advocate who understands appellate procedure to dismantle the appellant's arguments and protect your hard-won victory.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq r border border-line bg-card">
          <summary class="flex min-h-14 cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 px-5 py-4 font-serif text-lg leading-snug">
            <span>Why shouldn't I just use my trial lawyer for the appeal?</span><span class="faq-icon shrink-0 text-accent-text" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          </summary>
          <p class="max-w-[40rem] px-5 pb-5 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted sm:text-base">Trial attorneys excel at presenting evidence, questioning witnesses, and arguing before a judge. Appellate attorneys specialize in legal research, identifying procedural flaws, and writing, and if necessary, arguing before a panel of appellate judges. A fresh, objective perspective is often exactly what is needed to present the trial court errors in a persuasive manner.</p>
        </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<style>
  .faq summary::-webkit-details-marker { display: none; }
  .faq-icon { position: relative; width: 1rem; height: 1rem; }
  .faq-icon::before, .faq-icon::after { content: ""; position: absolute; left: 0; top: 50%; width: 100%; height: 1.5px; background: currentColor; transition: transform 0.3s cubic-bezier(0.16,1,0.3,1); }
  .faq-icon::after { transform: rotate(90deg); }
  .faq[open] .faq-icon::after { transform: rotate(0); }
</style>
